  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nEarly morning water vapor imagery shows the quick moving shortwave\nover the northern Rockies that is expected to bring rain chances\nacross the state later this afternoon and overnight. In the meantime,\nanother day of mild highs in the 50s is expected before rain spreads\ninto north central Kansas this afternoon and into eastern Kansas by\nlate evening. Most guidance indicating .25 to .5 inches of\nprecipitation, although have increased amounts on the higher side of\nguidance in a swath from southwest to northeast across the forecast\narea where better forcing and residence time for lift may increase\namounts. Back side of the system brings below freezing temperatures\nin the mid levels, but at this time the surface warm layer has\ndeepened enough that rain appears to be the predominant precipitation\ntype. If we are to see rain mixed with snow it would be after\nmidnight as the column cools through the night. While the strongest\nwinds look to remain over southwestern and southern Kansas, sustained\nwinds of 15-20 mph and gusts of 30-40 mph move through with the\nsystem in the overnight hours before the gradient lightens up mid \nmorning. \n\nAfter a colder day on Sunday, conditions mid week moderate back to\nmild conditions for early December, until the next chance for rain \ncomes through sometime Thursday.",
